    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the formula. can u explain the phase shift part a bit more? like why does the current lead the voltage in a capacitor? i'm having a hard time visualizing how that actually works in the circuit.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The phase shift in a capacitor, where the current leads the voltage, is a fundamental concept in AC circuits. Imagine the capacitor plates—they store charge, and current is the flow of charge. When the AC voltage starts to rise, the capacitor begins to charge, meaning the current must flow *before* the voltage across it reaches its peak. This is because the capacitor needs time to build up the opposing charge. It's like trying to get a bucket full of water (the voltage) to fill up; the flow of water (the current) has to start *before* the bucket is completely full.",
      "source": "target",
