      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, there are factors that limit the charging rate, though the primary limitation in an ideal capacitor is its inherent capacitance itself. The rate at which charge moves is fundamentally governed by the capacitance ($C$) and the applied voltage, which determines the charge ($Q = CV$). In a real-world scenario, the *resistance* of the wires or the internal resistance of the capacitor itself (ESR) will definitely limit how quickly the current can flow, which is why we often look at the RC time constant ($\\tau = RC$). This time constant dictates how long it takes for the capacitor to reach a certain percentage of its final charge.",
